WEBVTT

NOTE
Generated by scripts/song-video/render.js from the song storyboard in _data/songs/; edit that file, not this one.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.211
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:13.473
(narrator) The intro: a student types ucla.edu into a browser window floating in a starry sky, and a beam reaches a castle on a floating rock, the server.

3
00:00:13.473 --> 00:00:17.286
(narrator) The sky fills with machines, and a gold ring picks out the castle.

4
00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:24.219
(narrator) Then four colored layers, Application, Transport, Internet, and Link, each with its job, and the title: Unbox the stack.

5
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:26.033
[music]

6
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:29.913
You type ucla.edu and hit enter.

7
00:00:30.293 --> 00:00:34.493
Half a second later: the page. How'd it find the right machine?

8
00:00:35.513 --> 00:00:40.873
Four layers. Each solves one problem, so the others don't have to.

9
00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:42.513
Let's unbox it.

10
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.508
(narrator) Verse 1: an HTTP GET request flies out of the browser.

11
00:00:46.508 --> 00:00:50.651
(narrator) A DNS panel turns the name ucla.edu into the number 3.33.167.235.

12
00:00:50.651 --> 00:00:59.765
(narrator) Then the request is wrapped in one box per layer: a TCP box with ports, an IP box from the laptop’s address to the server’s, and a Wi-Fi frame.

13
00:00:59.765 --> 00:01:10.983
(narrator) The boxed packet travels across router islands, getting a new frame at every hop, and finally the headers and payload are pointed out, and a big page splits into three packets.

14
00:01:11.587 --> 00:01:14.607
♪ You hit enter. Your browser writes a GET: ♪

15
00:01:14.627 --> 00:01:17.067
♪ HTTP, “Send me the page.” That’s the request. ♪

16
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.827
♪ Routers can’t use names, so DNS gets the call: ♪

17
00:01:19.827 --> 00:01:22.487
♪ name in, IP out, the contacts app for all. ♪

18
00:01:22.487 --> 00:01:25.767
♪ Down to Transport: TCP adds a header, ♪

19
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:28.167
♪ port to port, so the right app’s gonna get it. ♪

20
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.707
♪ Down to Internet: IP wraps it one more time, ♪

21
00:01:30.707 --> 00:01:33.447
♪ “from my IP to yours,” machine to machine, worldwide. ♪

22
00:01:33.447 --> 00:01:36.527
♪ Down to Link: a Wi-Fi frame, one hop to your router. ♪

23
00:01:36.527 --> 00:01:39.267
♪ Each router swaps the frame and sends it further. ♪

24
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:42.047
♪ Each header’s a label for its layer. The payload’s the stuff. ♪

25
00:01:42.047 --> 00:01:45.433
♪ Routers only need the outer labels. Too big? Split it up. ♪

26
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:51.381
(narrator) The chorus shows three stacks: your laptop, a router with only the Internet and Link layers, and the server.

27
00:01:51.381 --> 00:01:58.912
(narrator) Each layer lights up as it is sung, with arcs showing who it talks to: one hop, machine to machine, app to app, and the language the apps speak.

28
00:01:58.912 --> 00:02:03.567
(narrator) Then a packet rides down one stack gaining a box per layer, and up the other losing them.

29
00:02:04.173 --> 00:02:08.773
♪ Link Layer: one hop on the local network. Ethernet, Wi-Fi. ♪

30
00:02:08.773 --> 00:02:12.713
♪ Internet Layer: machine to machine, across networks. IP. ♪

31
00:02:12.713 --> 00:02:17.953
♪ Transport Layer: app to app, reliable or fast. TCP or UDP. ♪

32
00:02:17.953 --> 00:02:20.453
♪ Application Layer: the language the apps speak. HTTP. ♪

33
00:02:20.453 --> 00:02:23.293
♪ Wrap it on the way down, unbox it on the way up. ♪

34
00:02:23.293 --> 00:02:26.533
♪ Each layer only uses the one below. That’s the stack! ♪

35
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:31.280
(narrator) Verse 2: TCP, with a shield, and UDP, with a lightning bolt, split at a fork.

36
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.478
(narrator) A sequence diagram shows the handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, with starting sequence numbers.

37
00:02:36.478 --> 00:02:45.777
(narrator) Numbered packets reach a receive buffer out of order and are sorted by number; ACKs travel back, and lost or broken packets are sent again when a timer runs out.

38
00:02:45.777 --> 00:02:54.440
(narrator) FIN, FIN-ACK, ACK close the connection, and a receipt lists the price of safe: a round trip of hellos, extra ACKs, and a lost piece blocking the rest.

39
00:02:55.047 --> 00:02:57.907
♪ Transport’s got two vibes. Your app picks the path: ♪

40
00:02:57.907 --> 00:03:00.567
♪ TCP plays it safe. UDP plays it fast. ♪

41
00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:03.167
♪ TCP shakes hands first, three steps in a row: ♪

42
00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:05.967
♪ SYN: “you up?” SYN-ACK: “yeah, you?” ACK: “bet.” Let’s go. ♪

43
00:03:05.967 --> 00:03:08.687
♪ SYN means “sync”: both sides share where their count begins, ♪

44
00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.567
♪ so every byte is numbered and sorted at the end. ♪

45
00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:14.247
♪ What lands gets ACKed: “got it,” like a read receipt. ♪

46
00:03:14.247 --> 00:03:17.167
♪ No ACK in time? Left on read. Resend that piece. ♪

47
00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:20.007
♪ Broken checksum? Tossed, no ACK, so it’s sent once more. ♪

48
00:03:20.007 --> 00:03:22.887
♪ Done? FIN, FIN-ACK, ACK: free the memory, close the door. ♪

49
00:03:22.887 --> 00:03:25.467
♪ But safe has a price. Here’s what you pay: ♪

50
00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:28.407
♪ a round trip of hellos before your data’s on its way, ♪

51
00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.407
♪ extra ACKs along the way, ♪

52
00:03:30.407 --> 00:03:33.900
♪ and one lost piece holds up the line. The rest has to wait. ♪

53
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.810
(narrator) Verse 3, in pink: UDP skips the handshake, and its header has two rows to TCP’s five.

54
00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:44.071
(narrator) Unnumbered packets arrive out of order, with no ACKs and no resend; broken or lost ones are gone.

55
00:03:44.071 --> 00:03:49.006
(narrator) Two game maps compare sending your position with sending your moves when an update is lost.

56
00:03:49.006 --> 00:03:55.406
(narrator) A live call shows a resent word arriving too late, and a DNS lookup gets one answer, with the app retrying on its own.

57
00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.840
♪ UDP skips the hello. The first packet’s data. ♪

58
00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.640
♪ Tiny header, no connection, nothing to remember later. ♪

59
00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.340
♪ No numbers, so no order. No ACKs, so no resend. ♪

60
00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.060
♪ Broken checksum? Tossed. Lost? Gone. That’s the end. ♪

61
00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.840
♪ So if losing one would hurt, your app needs a plan: ♪

62
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.660
♪ send “I’m at x, y,” not “I moved.” The next one shows where you stand. ♪

63
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.540
♪ Why use it? On a live call, late is as bad as lost: ♪

64
00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.400
♪ resend a voice packet, and it lands after the word is gone. ♪

65
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.980
♪ DNS? One tiny question, one tiny reply. ♪

66
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.333
♪ A handshake would double the wait. Lost? The app tries one more time. ♪

67
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:28.679
(narrator) The hook: two glowing gates, TCP and UDP, each with its rule.

68
00:04:28.679 --> 00:04:43.732
(narrator) Needs and use cases fly into the gate they belong to: every byte in order, DMs, logins, files, and web pages into TCP; old news, calls, game moves, and quick lookups into UDP; a buffered video into TCP and a live call into UDP.

69
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.800
♪ Need every byte, in order, even if you wait? TCP! ♪

70
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.840
♪ Old news is useless? Keep it live: UDP! ♪

71
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.440
♪ DMs, logins, files, web pages: TCP! ♪

72
00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.400
♪ Calls, game moves, quick lookups: UDP! ♪

73
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.020
♪ A video that buffers can wait for a resend: TCP. ♪

74
00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.333
♪ A live call can’t wait: UDP. ♪

75
00:05:02.633 --> 00:05:08.377
(narrator) The chorus again: the three stacks light up layer by layer, and a packet is wrapped on the way down and unboxed on the way up.

76
00:05:08.967 --> 00:05:13.087
♪ Link Layer: one hop on the local network. Ethernet, Wi-Fi. ♪

77
00:05:13.087 --> 00:05:17.187
♪ Internet Layer: machine to machine, across networks. IP. ♪

78
00:05:17.187 --> 00:05:22.527
♪ Transport Layer: app to app, reliable or fast. TCP or UDP. ♪

79
00:05:22.527 --> 00:05:25.107
♪ Application Layer: the language the apps speak. HTTP. ♪

80
00:05:25.107 --> 00:05:27.747
♪ Wrap it on the way down, unbox it on the way up. ♪

81
00:05:27.747 --> 00:05:30.933
♪ Each layer only uses the one below. That’s the stack! ♪

82
00:05:31.233 --> 00:05:41.593
(narrator) Verse 4: the laptop’s address, 192.168.1.42, splits into a network part, the city, and a host part, the street; each of its four numbers runs from 0 to 255.

83
00:05:41.593 --> 00:05:46.839
(narrator) A counter passes four billion, stamped not enough, and an IPv6 address appears.

84
00:05:46.839 --> 00:05:51.488
(narrator) A loop leaves the student’s laptop and comes straight back: localhost.

85
00:05:51.488 --> 00:05:57.996
(narrator) The castle’s windows are apps with port numbers, and packets climb to the window their port names.

86
00:05:57.996 --> 00:06:01.914
(narrator) Last, a full router drops a packet, and TCP sends it again.

87
00:06:02.527 --> 00:06:05.167
♪ Internet Layer: every machine gets an IP address, ♪

88
00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:08.407
♪ network part, then host part: city, then street. No guessing. ♪

89
00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:10.887
♪ IPv4: four numbers, zero to 255. ♪

90
00:06:10.887 --> 00:06:14.047
♪ Four billion addresses? Not enough. So IPv6 arrived. ♪

91
00:06:14.047 --> 00:06:16.467
♪ 127.0.0.1? ♪

92
00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:18.927
♪ There’s no place like home: localhost, your own machine. ♪

93
00:06:18.927 --> 00:06:21.947
♪ IP finds the machine, but one machine runs lots of apps, ♪

94
00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:24.707
♪ so the port picks the app: 3000, 443, like that. ♪

95
00:06:24.707 --> 00:06:27.347
♪ IP does its best, but packets still get dropped, ♪

96
00:06:27.347 --> 00:06:30.233
♪ and IP won’t resend. That’s TCP’s job. Full stop. ♪

97
00:06:30.533 --> 00:06:35.700
(narrator) The bridge: requests fly to the server, and a note shows it remembers nothing between them.

98
00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.198
(narrator) A request without a token gets: who are you.

99
00:06:38.198 --> 00:06:42.115
(narrator) One that brings everything is answered by any of three server copies.

100
00:06:42.115 --> 00:06:48.190
(narrator) Then HTTPS: a login request is locked in a TLS box and scrambles, so someone on café Wi-Fi sees only noise.

101
00:06:48.793 --> 00:06:53.353
♪ Up top, HTTP has no chat history. ♪

102
00:06:53.353 --> 00:06:58.993
♪ Every request starts fresh. The server won’t remember me, ♪

103
00:06:58.993 --> 00:07:05.093
♪ so each request brings all it needs, and any server copy can reply. ♪

104
00:07:05.253 --> 00:07:08.213
♪ HTTPS: HTTP locked inside TLS. ♪

105
00:07:08.213 --> 00:07:10.667
♪ Nobody on the Wi-Fi reads your passwords. ♪

106
00:07:10.967 --> 00:07:16.826
(narrator) The pop quiz: six cards, each with a picture from the song, a bar that fills while you think, and then the answer.

107
00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.180
♪ Pop quiz! One: going down the stack, each layer adds a what? ♪

108
00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.060
♪ (crowd) A header! ♪

109
00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.800
♪ IP finds the machine. What finds the app? ♪

110
00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:25.620
♪ (crowd) The port! ♪

111
00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.920
♪ Three: live voice call. TCP or UDP? ♪

112
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.960
♪ (crowd) UDP! Old news is useless! ♪

113
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.500
♪ A packet’s lost. TCP? ♪

114
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.580
♪ (crowd) Resend it! The rest wait! ♪

115
00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:35.360
♪ And UDP? ♪

116
00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.180
♪ (crowd) Gone! No resend! ♪

117
00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.600
♪ What’s HTTPS? ♪

118
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.533
♪ (crowd) Secure HTTP inside TLS! ♪

119
00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:49.231
(narrator) The final chorus: the three stacks light up layer by layer once more, and a packet is wrapped on the way down and unboxed on the way up.

120
00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:53.960
♪ Link Layer: one hop on the local network. Ethernet, Wi-Fi. ♪

121
00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.140
♪ Internet Layer: machine to machine, across networks. IP. ♪

122
00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.180
♪ Transport Layer: app to app, reliable or fast. TCP or UDP. ♪

123
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.640
♪ Application Layer: the language the apps speak. HTTP. ♪

124
00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.480
♪ Wrap it on the way down, unbox it on the way up. ♪

125
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.300
♪ Each layer only uses the one below. That’s the stack! ♪

126
00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.194
(narrator) The outro: 1969, Boelter Hall at UCLA.

127
00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:21.233
(narrator) Two steel cabinets called IMPs, the first two nodes of the net, are joined by a leased line.

128
00:08:21.233 --> 00:08:25.303
(narrator) The letters L and O travel from UCLA to SRI, then SRI crashes.

129
00:08:25.303 --> 00:08:31.080
(narrator) Name cards for Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn frame the words TCP slash IP and their 1974 paper.

130
00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.559
(narrator) Last: Go Bruins, the full stack lit up, and confetti.

131
00:08:35.137 --> 00:08:41.147
1969, Boelter Hall: Kleinrock’s lab, the first node on the net.

132
00:08:41.447 --> 00:08:45.607
The first message said “LO,” then it crashed. Don’t forget.

133
00:08:45.667 --> 00:08:52.167
Vint Cerf, a grad student there, later co-designed TCP/IP with Bob Kahn.

134
00:08:52.527 --> 00:08:55.007
Go Bruins! Now you know the stack.

135
00:08:55.567 --> 00:08:57.307
That’s HTTP!

136
00:08:57.307 --> 00:08:59.607
[music]

137
00:08:59.607 --> 00:09:00.827
Secure!

138
00:09:00.827 --> 00:09:07.567
[music]
